<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- AD HOC / BASE entitlements. This file deliberately carries NONE of
       com.apple.application-identifier, com.apple.developer.team-identifier
       or keychain-access-groups. Those are RESTRICTED: macOS honours them only
       when an embedded Developer ID provisioning profile grants them, and a
       binary that claims them without the profile is killed at exec (SIGKILL,
       exit 137). The ad hoc dev build has no profile, so adding them here
       would make every local build die at launch.

       They live in LDA-distribution.entitlements, which package-app.sh uses
       only when CODESIGN_IDENTITY and PROVISIONING_PROFILE are both set and the
       profile has been copied into the bundle. PackagingEntitlementsTests
       pins both facts: this file has none of the three, and the distribution
       file is exactly this file plus those three. -->

  <!-- App Sandbox on. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Read and write the documents the user explicitly opens or exports,
       granted through the open/save panels (powerbox). -->
  <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Persist access to a model the user picked in an open panel, so it is
       still readable after the next launch. Without this the sandbox grants
       access to the URL for one launch only, the stored path silently stops
       resolving, and the app falls back to a different model while the user
       believes their choice is still in effect.

       NOTE FOR FUTURE EDITORS: this is a FILE bookmark entitlement, not a
       network one. It grants no outbound or inbound access of any kind and
       does not weaken the guarantee described below. Do not remove it while
       "tidying up" entitlements: doing so reintroduces a silent redaction
       defect that testing on an unsandboxed dev build cannot catch. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Outbound network, for ONE purpose: downloading model files the user has
       explicitly asked for in Settings, then AI, then Manage Models.

       This entitlement was deliberately absent until the Model Management
       feature. The previous comment claimed the app never touches the network;
       that is no longer true and the claim has been corrected everywhere it
       appeared, rather than left standing while the behaviour changed
       underneath it.

       What is still guaranteed, and what a security review can verify:
       - Document content NEVER leaves this machine. Detection, redaction and
         restoration run entirely on-device against a local GGUF file. No
         document text, entity list, mapping or filename is ever transmitted.
       - The only outbound requests are model downloads to the host named in
         Models.json, and they happen only when the user starts one.
       - There is no telemetry, no analytics, no crash reporting, no update
         check.
       - .network.server is still absent: nothing can connect INTO the app.

       If you are auditing this: grep the sources for URLSession. Every use must
       sit behind Model Management. Any other use is a defect, not a feature. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- If notarization ever rejects the Metal/ggml binary for memory protection,
       uncomment the following (allow-jit is the least-privilege option):
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key><true/>
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
